    "body": "Thanks so much for taking a look!!\r\n\r\n> I did an initial pass. I think this definitely needs review from multiple people given the size.\r\n\r\nI've enrolled @jakearchibald to help :)\r\n\r\n> Can download the hyperlink still be reconciled with the navigate algorithm? (Looks like yes.)\r\n\r\nYep, this doesn't significantly change things, and ensures anything added to navigation is also added to downloading.\r\n\r\n> Why \"classic\" for \"classic history API state\" and not \"legacy\"?\r\n\r\nSimilarly with classic scripts, it felt too early to call the thing people are using a lot in today's code \"legacy\". I don't mind changing it though, if you'd prefer :).\r\n\r\n> Nit: I find the three letter variable names rather confusing. I would prefer sessionHistoryEntry or entry over she, for instance.\r\n\r\nI sympathize. However for algorithms where we mix the session history entry specification type, and the `NavigationHistoryEntry` IDL type, it feels like what I have is the best path? (Most of these are in https://whatpr.org/html/8502/nav-history-apis.html#navigation-api-entry-updates .)\r\n\r\nWe could change it to use \"entry\" for session history entries and \"NHE\" for `NavigationHistoryEntry`s, maybe? Open to suggestions.\r\n\r\n> The NavigateEvent's type attribute is never initialized. (This is a bit concerning as I guess it means the implementation wasn't written based on the specification.)\r\n\r\nIt is; see \"inner navigate event firing algorithm\" step 9.\r\n\r\n> \"inner navigate event firing algorithm\" has a lot of magic around influencing the normal order of the event loop. Is this really unique to navigation or is there a deeper issue here?\r\n\r\nI think it's not unique to navigation, but might be exotic enough to have never occured before. The reason this comes up is that we have an algorithm that:\r\n\r\n- Sometimes is called with an empty JS stack (in our case, traversal) and sometimes with a full JS stack (non-traverse navigation);\r\n- Both fires events and resolves promises;\r\n- And we generally expect developers to use both those events and promises, and expect a consistent ordering between them.\r\n\r\nThis _sort of_ comes up with event handlers and users clicking (empty stack) vs. `click()` (no clicking), as discussed in https://jakearchibald.com/2015/tasks-microtasks-queues-and-schedules/. But there's no platform-generated promises in that case. If we added some sort of `document.nextClick` promise, then maybe we'd be in a similar situation. And in that case we might want to think about abstracting things. But for now I think it's OK to do this as a one-off.\r\n\r\n> \"user navigation involvement\" seems problematic in that isTrusted can be true for events dispatched by the user agent. [..] It might be okay for a limited set of call sites, but then we ought to make that clear somehow. Perhaps by inlining it.\r\n\r\nFair point; I'll have a think about the best way to do this.\r\n\r\n> This doesn't impact BFCache, right?\r\n\r\nIt doesn't prevent bfcaching, or have any ongoing processes that need to be shut down or managed during bfcaching. But it does have one interesting interaction, which is cases like this:\r\n\r\n1. Session history is A -> B -> C -> D, you are currently on B.\r\n2. You move to C, and keep B around in bfcache.\r\n3. You do `history.pushState()` or `navigation.navigate()` or anything else, blowing away D and replacing it with E.\r\n4. You do `history.back()` or `navigation.back()`, sending you back to B, reactivating it from bfcache.\r\n\r\nWe need to ensure that `navigation.entries()` contains A -> B -> C -> E, not its old value of A -> B -> C -> D. Also we need to ensure a `dispose` event is fired for D. This is accomplished in \"update the entries for reactivation\".",
